{
  "_doc": "Kinship as a shared frame (Phase 009). Two layers. GENEALOGY is a world fact: bore/fathered (parent to child) and union_with edges with their periods, recorded in the world graph. KIN_TERMS are what people call one another; a resident HOLDS a term as a private reference, seeded from genealogy at founding and changed later only by the resident's backend. A term can be held without a genealogical path (see KIN_ATTRIBUTION).\n\nKIN_TERMS: generation (+1 up, -1 down, 0 level); sex (null = either); reciprocal (the term the other person holds); paths (step sequences from the holder: parent = up to a parent, child = down to a child, spouse = an active union partner, sibling = another child of one of my parents; a path is a term's usual genealogical route, for example uncle = parent's sibling, or parent's sister's husband); shared_parents (optional filter on a sibling step: 'both' full siblings, 'one' half siblings); obligations (KIN_OBLIGATIONS the term normally implies); attributions (KIN_ATTRIBUTION kinds that can give the term without a path). A term with no paths arises only by attribution. The first matching term in table order is the one held.\n\nKIN_ATTRIBUTION: the ways a term is held WITHOUT the genealogical path. 'terms' are the KIN_TERMS it can give; 'inherits' says whether the holder counts as a heir under a custom (only adoption does, by default). These are model categories; the village's real practice varies (APPROX).\n\nKIN_OBLIGATIONS: what a kin term normally implies, as frames a resident may hold. They are social norms, not world rules.\n\nINHERITANCE_CUSTOM: the default custom used to publish genealogical heirs when a resident dies. 'default' names the custom in use. Each custom has 'spouse' ('share' = a spouse takes an equal share in the first tier, 'life_interest' = the spouse is listed with no share of the estate) and 'tiers' in order: the first tier with living members lists them, sharing under 'rule' ('equal' splits equally, 'eldest' gives the whole to the eldest by birth). Publishing a heir list never moves property; it feeds the existing 'inheritance' deed type.\n\nSources: Goody, Thirsk and Thompson (eds.), Family and Inheritance: Rural Society in Western Europe 1200-1800 (Cambridge UP, 1976), which describes both partible and impartible customary inheritance in English and continental villages. The default (partible) is a model choice, not a measured village rule (APPROX). Kin term definitions are the usual English genealogical usage; the step, godparent, and honorific terms are social categories with no fixed genealogical path (APPROX).",
